nd the CIA or both of  which were feathering their own nest in  terms of of what they wanted to be made  public and what would be embarrassing to  them there was no way and the CIA had a  member of the Warren Commission so the  Warren Commission was acting like  McNamara was acting about Vietnam bright  people taking the evidence before them  drawing a perfectly appropriate  conclusion but if the evidence is wrong  you draw their own conclusions  so the Warren Commission did  have the information subsequently  different people were dead and you know  Giancana was killed shortly before he  testified before the house assassination  committee shot in the neck and mouth  which is the month the Mafia's way of  saying you'll never speak again and  trafficante died Hoffa was in prison  Hoffa and and Marcello tried to bribe  witnesses to get half a head of prison  to say that the testimony against him  was false so they were they were the  people were never together at the same  time that the evidence was accumulated  and and and the evidence still can  continues to build will probably never  have the decisive evidence that we might  have it'll be interesting to see when  Fidel Castro dies and those records are  completely made public some have been to  see what would happen but there were  there were these plots and plans going  on right up until the very moment of the  assassination somebody from the CIA was  meeting in Paris with a Cuban who they  were using to kill Castro and three days  before the assassination Castro said to  an AP reporter at a meeting in Havana I  know that they're trying to get me and  if they get me it's their leaders are  going to be gotten as well so all of  this stuff was going on roughly at the  same time but but but none of it was  known when the Warren Commission issued  its reports  do you think the Warren Commission  report the best job they could have her  I knew the people who were working I  think they were earnest I don't think I  don'